"site-nav-button usage" is a notebook page
	<header level="1">
		Site nav button
		<header>A site nav button shows or hides the side navigation of the page layout around it:  its icons only and back on a wide page, slid in and out on a narrow one.</>
	</>
	<section>
		<toolbar class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, framework snippets), keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Put it at the start of the top bar of a `<ui-page-layout>`, in its `menu` slot:
			it finds the layout around it, across shadow roots, and calls its `toggleDrawer()`.
			A Spell site's page layout writes a bare `<site-nav-button slot="start" />` in its app menu.

			- It takes no attributes of its own:  an item's, for its look in a menu
			  (its `icon`, `aria-label` and `title` replace its own).
			- With one inside it, the layout draws no menu button of its own at a narrow width.
			- Outside a menu, it's a small basic button with the same icon.
			- The layout sends `ui-toggle` as the drawer changes:
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			document.querySelector("ui-page-layout").addEventListener("ui-toggle", (event) => {
			  console.log("the drawer is now", event.detail.newState)
			})
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			It's a button:  Tab reaches it, Enter or Space presses it.
			In a menu bar, the bar's arrow keys reach it too.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			Its name and tooltip are "Show or hide the side bar", unless the page gives its own `aria-label` and `title`;
			`aria-expanded` says whether the drawer shows all of it.
		</>
	</>
